# Domain-driven bypass for mango-vpnguard's `direct` policy route.
#
#   sudo install -m 0644 /usr/local/share/mango-vpnguard/dnsmasq.d-mango-vpnguard.conf.example \
#        /etc/NetworkManager/dnsmasq.d/mango-vpnguard.conf
#
# then put your own domains in the copy. Nothing here ships filled in: a
# domain list is a site value, and this repo holds none.
#
# What it does. Every answer for a listed domain puts its A record into the
# `bypass4` set in the ruleset's `inet vpn` table. The `marks` chain then
# stamps 0x1 on packets to that address, and the `direct` rule sends them out
# the physical uplink instead of the tunnel. Entries inherit the set's own 1h
# timeout, so they expire with the answer that created them rather than
# collecting for ever.
#
# Why per answer, and not a script that resolves the list on a timer: a site
# behind a CDN rotates its addresses faster than any refresh interval, so a
# list resolved on a timer is a list that is wrong most of the time.
#
# Syntax is dnsmasq's own:  /<domain>/<domain>/.../4#<family>#<table>#<set>
# The leading `4#` keeps AAAA answers out — `bypass4` holds IPv4, and without
# it every AAAA answer logs an error.

#nftset=/example.com/example.net/4#inet#vpn#bypass4

# After editing, restart NetworkManager's dnsmasq so it re-reads this file.
# This does not drop the Wi-Fi association:
#
#   sudo nmcli general reload dns-full
